            'body_en' => "The Romans knew it as Cepha or Kiphas, a fortress of legionaries on the frontier with Persia; a bishop from here sat at the Council of Chalcedon in 451; the Arabs took it around 640 and called it Hisn Kayfa, \"the rock fortress\". But the town that mattered was the one built after 1101, when the Artuqid Turkmen made it their capital. They threw a bridge across the Tigris, raised a palace on the citadel rock a hundred metres above the water, and ruled a court that al-Jazari — the engineer whose book of ingenious devices includes the first programmable machines — served from here in the twelfth century.\n\nThe Ayyubids, Saladin\'s dynasty, took the city in 1232 and built its mosques and madrasas; the Aq Qoyunlu followed in 1462 and left the tomb of Zeynel Bey, a cylinder of brick glazed turquoise and navy on the opposite bank. Below and around all this, thousands of rooms were cut into the soft limestone — houses, stables, churches, a whole second town in the rock, lived in by Kurdish, Arab, Armenian and Syriac families until the 1970s and 80s.\n\nIn 1981 Turkey declared it a conservation area. In 2006 it began the Ilısu Dam downstream. On 1 April 2020 the reservoir reached 498 metres; by midsummer the old town, the caves and the bridge piers were under the lake. A new Hasankeyf had been built on the plateau, and eight monuments — the tomb, a mosque, a bath, a tower — had been cut from their foundations and carried uphill.",
